WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome back to the&nbsp;
program today, Democratic Congressman Ro Khanna,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:10.240
who represents California's 17th District. It's&nbsp;
great to have you back on. And it's you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.880
the last time you were on was before the&nbsp;
Russian invasion of Ukraine. So I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:20.560
maybe we would start there. Weeks ago, there&nbsp;
were reports, seemingly credible reports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:26.160
that Russia sort of had the resources for about&nbsp;
another ten days of what they started with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.640
It's obviously been more than ten days. And what's&nbsp;
taking place on the ground has shifted to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.600
degree. What's your outlook now based on what&nbsp;
you've been briefed on that you can talk about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.720
about what the longevity of&nbsp;
this might potentially be?&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:46.160
Speaker 4: I don't underestimate the Russians. In&nbsp;
fact, Charles Lester had sent me an article, is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:55.440
expert on what Russia did in Syria, just reminding&nbsp;
us how brutal Russia can be. And they spent almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:04.800
a decade in Syria where they did in Aleppo,&nbsp;
what they're doing now, two cities in Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:14.000
and they've had the same record in Georgia. So I&nbsp;
admire and support unconditionally the Ukrainian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:22.720
resistance, but I don't think we should be a&nbsp;
rose eyed in terms of how difficult the this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.880
is going to be and how ruthless Russia is.
Speaker 1: There are some, including to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:36.560
degree Noam Chomsky and others who have focused&nbsp;
on the what they see as the futility of resistance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:43.760
against the overwhelming power of Russia, and&nbsp;
that Ukraine should essentially give Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.600
some stuff and try to end hostilities as quickly&nbsp;
as possible. Not a perspective that many others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:53.600
There are many others who very much&nbsp;
disagree with that perspective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:00.080
What's your sense of what Ukraine should be doing?
Speaker 4: Well, my job as an American lawmaker is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.880
to support Ukraine and to provide make sure&nbsp;
we're providing them with military weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.400
which we are, and make sure that they're&nbsp;
getting the resources that they need. Make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.240
sure that the president's wolanski has the&nbsp;
unconditional support of the united states.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.600
I think the president has done a tremendous&nbsp;
job with secretary blinken in rallying naito.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.560
I have called out countries like india because&nbsp;
i'm the vice chair of the india caucusing. We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:29.280
got to be unified in condemning Putin. But I also&nbsp;
have said that we have to, at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:36.720
have a negotiated settlement and the United States&nbsp;
should be working towards that to do whatever we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:43.200
can before that. But strengthening Ukraine and&nbsp;
having this resistance succeed, I think, is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:49.840
step to that, because it weakens Putin's hand. If&nbsp;
Putin has to be in a weak position to even accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:58.000
some compromise, I mean, Zelensky has floated&nbsp;
compromises. He has said he's open to seeing, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:04.600
even not even being neutral in certain cases.&nbsp;
But Putin is the one, as my understanding is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.320
and coming to the table, both.
Speaker 1: In terms of U.S. troops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.440
on the ground. Joe Biden from the beginning said&nbsp;
that that's not something that he's willing to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:21.840
and there's significant support for that, but&nbsp;
not total support for that red line. What's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.880
your position on on boots on the ground?
